; 38I — the forge is the first business in the game the player can own a piece of, and it is a two-rung
; ladder rather than a single payment so the sink has somewhere to keep going. Bryn is the right first
; merchant for it: a forge is a thing with capacity, and buying into one reads as buying more of it.
;
; Each rung does the same two things, which is all a stake ever does: it raises what he can pay for a
; haul (PurseBonus, applied at every future restock, permanently) and it opens a shelf he otherwise
; keeps back. It moves no price — standing already owns the price ramp (38C), and 38F's sweep contract
; means a third multiplier would have to earn its place in the invariant test. This one has nothing to
; add to it.
;
; The costs (600 then 1500) are late-game money against a 300g purse and a 30g sword. They are meant to
; be a decision rather than a purchase, and like every number here they are Phase 56's to tune.

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Invest_apprentice"]
script = ExtResource("3_tier")
Cost = 600
PurseBonus = 150

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Invest_partner"]
script = ExtResource("3_tier")
Cost = 1500
PurseBonus = 300

; The two gated shelves. A ring first (cheap enough that the first rung nearly pays for itself in
; access), the drakescale mail second — the most valuable thing any merchant in the Ember Crown sells,
; and the one piece of gear in town that is bought rather than looted or forged.
;
; Both stay VISIBLE and greyed while locked, with the gate named in the tooltip. A hidden row teaches the
; player nothing; a locked one is the entire reason they find out a stake buys something.

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Stock_ring"]
script = ExtResource("2_entry")
ItemId = "item.ring.iron"
Quantity = 1
RequiredInvestment = 1

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Stock_mail"]
script = ExtResource("2_entry")
ItemId = "item.armor.drakescale_mail"
Quantity = 1
RequiredInvestment = 2

